money market funds are really just  open-ended mutual funds but instead of  investing in stocks they invest in  really safe loans and Theora gets really  safe bonds and t-bills  so here's let's say that this is the  asset side of our money market fund and  right over here is the equity side we'll  assume it has no liabilities and the  value the total value of this equity is  often referred to in mutual fund world  as the net asset value so if this was  the this was divided into a million  shares you would take the nav divide it  into a million shares and you would have  your net asset value per share now this  structure that I just drew here is true  for any mutual fund but when we're  talking about money market funds we're  actually talking about what they're  investing in here on this asset side so  on a money market mutual fund they'll  invest in things like Treasury bills  which are really just loans to the  federal government that will mature in a  year or less or that I guess you could  view it as the lender is going to get  paid back in a year or less or  commercial paper and commercial paper is  just another word for short-term debt to  really really really safe corporations  and banks so commercial paper and just  to be clear t-bills mature in one year  or less and commercial paper matures in  one to two hundred and seventy days so  the reason why it's called the money  market is because it's the short-term  debt you're lending to these safe  entities on a very short-term basis just  that they can meet there I guess you  could say their cash requirements or  their money requirements but in general  these type of mutual funds these money  market funds are considered to be super  duper duper safe because besides the  fact that these are safe people to lend  to you're also going to be getting your  money pretty soon and these are also  super liquid markets so for whatever  reason even if even if you can't wait  for the 30 days until that commercial  paper matures it's very likely that it's  a very liquid market and you could  probably sell that security and get a  good value for it so the general reason  why people invest in money market funds  is kind of an  turn ative to savings I can buy a share  of of one of these open-ended funds I'll  get a little bit of interest on it it'll  probably be better interest than I would  get in my savings or checking account  and it is at least from the point of  view of many many investors comparably  comparably safe
 

